    "lawCode": "Add §227-e, RP L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2949\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property law, in relation to requiring land-\nlords to mitigate damages if a tenant vacates a premises in violation of\nthe terms of the lease\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would require residential landlords to mitigate damages to a\ntenant in cases where the tenant vacated the premises.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill adds a new section 227-e of the real property\nlaw as it relates to imposing a duty on landlords to mitigate damages to\ntenants. The landlord must, in good faith, take reasonable actions to\nre-rent the premises at a fair market value. Any new tenant lease that\nis executed shall terminate the previous tenant's lease and any damages\nfrom that point forward.\n\nSection two of the bill relates to the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill will ensure that only actual damages are recoverable in situ-\nations where a tenant vacates a property in violation of the lease.\nCurrently, New York State law does not explicitly provide for the duty\nto mitigate damages on landlords.  There was a court decision that\napplied an earlier ruling related to commercial leases on residential\nleases; stating that the landlord has no obligation to mitigate damages\non a tenant (Rios v Carrillo, 2008). This decision states that if a\ntenant vacates the property in violation of the lease, the landlord can\nchoose not to move forward in re-renting the premises and allow the\ntenant to be responsible for damages equal to the entirety of the lease.\n\nThis bill would explicitly state that the landlord must take reasonable\nsteps to re-rent the premise. The tenant could still be liable for\ndamages if the premises cannot be re-rented or due to market consider-\nations had to be rented at a lower rent. Some judges already apply this\ngenerally accepted standard as a matter of equity, however, others do\nnot. This will provide the courts with guidance in determining damages\nin landlord-tenant cases.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
